   "id": "nc02",
   "category": "cross",
   "context": "1c255bf69f72dba2",
   "answer": "**Wait‑die aborts younger transactions, while wound‑wait aborts younger lock holders.**  \n\n### Mechanism  \n- **Wait‑die**: When a transaction requests a lock held by an older transaction, the younger transaction **waits**; if an older transaction requests a lock held by a younger one, the older transaction **dies** (is aborted) so the younger can continue [[1]].  \n- **Wound‑wait**: When an older transaction requests a lock held by a younger one, the older **wounds** (forces the younger to abort); a younger transaction that requests a lock held by an older one simply **waits** [[1]].\n\n### Effect on deadlocks  \nBoth schemes enforce a total ordering by timestamp, preventing circular wait cycles, but they differ in which transaction is chosen as the victim: wait‑die sacrifices the younger, wound‑wait sacrifices the younger lock holder.  \n\n**Key takeaway:** The two protocols use opposite abort policies—wait‑die aborts the requester if it’s younger, wound‑wait aborts the holder if it’s younger.",
